WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.246 --> 00:00:05.019
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.019 --> 00:00:09.896
Thanks for being here, and thanks for
the kind introduction, Jim.

00:00:09.896 --> 00:00:15.810
I wasn't planing to talk too much
about Unilever, if you don't mind.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:20.570
He said enough already about it,
so that's probably where it stops.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:26.757
But flying in this morning to come here,
I had to take an early plane,

00:00:26.757 --> 00:00:31.557
and I was sitting there at
the airport in Los Angeles,

00:00:31.557 --> 00:00:35.098
and I overheard three people talking.

00:00:35.098 --> 00:00:38.657
And I know it's very impolite to listen
to people talking at an airport, but

00:00:38.657 --> 00:00:40.113
I know you all do, so I'm not.

00:00:40.113 --> 00:00:42.620
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm not ashamed to share

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:43.527
the story with you.

00:00:43.527 --> 00:00:48.163
But I quickly discovered that there was
an engineer, an economist, and a doctor.

00:00:48.163 --> 00:00:51.033
And they were bragging,
and the doctor said,

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:54.061
hey, I have the oldest
profession in the world.

00:00:54.061 --> 00:00:56.210
And the others said, why is this?

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:57.904
He said, well,
you go to the Book of Genesis.

00:00:57.904 --> 00:01:01.590
Eve came out of the the rib from Adam,

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:04.440
and Adam came out of the ribs of Eve or
whoever.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.950
Both of them survived,
that was the first successful operation,

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.000
doctors have the oldest profession.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.500
And the engineer said, I don't think so.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.905
Because when there was chaos,
we created the universe, and

00:01:14.905 --> 00:01:17.994
to make order out of chaos,
you have to be an engineer.

00:01:17.994 --> 00:01:19.678
So engineers have the oldest profession.

00:01:19.678 --> 00:01:21.628
And the economist said, I don't think so,

00:01:21.628 --> 00:01:23.695
because who created chaos
in the first place?

00:01:23.695 --> 00:01:27.099
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I think that describes a little

00:01:27.099 --> 00:01:31.850
bit about what I want to talk about today,
who created chaos in the first place?

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.618
But before I do that, let me first
tell you how pleased I am to be

00:01:35.618 --> 00:01:39.040
here in this Conradin von
Gugelberg Memorial Lecture.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.715
We were just talking with some of
the people who have known him and

00:01:42.715 --> 00:01:47.480
studied with him in the class of 87, but
he seem to have been a remarkable person,

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.710
and frankly,
a little bit ahead of his time.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.720
As I said then,
I would love to met him, but

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:58.700
it's certainly an honor to be part of
his life indirectly by recognizing him.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.040
And doing that in a school like Stanford,
obviously, is a wonderful opportunity.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.250
We have a great institution here.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.344
Undoubtedly, I will call my mother
today and tell her I was at Stanford,

00:02:08.344 --> 00:02:09.526
and she'll be very proud.

00:02:09.526 --> 00:02:13.080
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But you also have a very progressive,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:18.980
and we were talking that vision towards
what the role of the education should be,

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:24.355
and the people that you are producing
here for tomorrow's wealth.

00:02:24.355 --> 00:02:26.060
He'd been very instrumental, as Jim said,

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:30.810
to work on the high common stock
issues around deforestation, etc.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:34.080
And we don't have the academic
world actively involved,

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.698
and I don't think we can solve so
many of the world's problems.

00:02:37.698 --> 00:02:41.496
We had a chance this morning,
with a tremendous group of professors and

00:02:41.496 --> 00:02:46.244
faculty and PhD students and others, to
talk a little bit about the challenges of

00:02:46.244 --> 00:02:49.114
in the supply chain,
which are not all that obvious.

00:02:49.114 --> 00:02:51.368
And to think about that together,

00:02:51.368 --> 00:02:55.799
the more you talk about it means
that these issues are solvable, but

00:02:55.799 --> 00:03:00.320
we all need to contribute our
own individual parts to this.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.660
And as a university, I think more than
ever, showing your leadership in the area

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:08.600
of sustainability will place you very
well, because the future issues that we

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:13.015
need to solve are obviously the challenges
of climate and energy or water or

00:03:13.015 --> 00:03:17.080
biodiversity or natural capital and
many of the other things.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.400
The students that are here,

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.790
I think the skill sets that we need
to learn to be successful in life,

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:28.988
undoubtedly, many of them are going
to be the same as we've always had.

00:03:28.988 --> 00:03:32.673
We need hardworking people, a certain
level of intelligence, integrity,

00:03:32.673 --> 00:03:34.669
all these things are not going to change.

00:03:34.669 --> 00:03:37.511
But increasingly,
we need to have some other skill sets to

00:03:37.511 --> 00:03:41.287
be successful in this world, and
clearly, many people struggle with that.

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:43.966
Otherwise, I believe we would
have solved already some

00:03:43.966 --> 00:03:45.791
of the challenges that are out there.

00:03:45.791 --> 00:03:49.874
But to be a good business person,
you need to know as much in the future,

00:03:49.874 --> 00:03:53.500
in my opinion, about sustainability
as you know about sales.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:57.942
So you need to know as much about climate
change as you do about cash flow, or

00:03:57.942 --> 00:04:02.174
you need to know as much about
international development as you do about

00:04:02.174 --> 00:04:03.671
business development.

00:04:03.671 --> 00:04:08.593
It really requires a broader
vision if you want to manage these

00:04:08.593 --> 00:04:13.530
challenges that we know we
are seeing everyday in this world.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.410
The first thing to start on
a little bit of a positive note,

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:19.710
I think it's actually
a great time to be here.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:24.720
If you look at the last 15 years, the
world has actually come quite a long way.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.940
We've seen the effects of
globalization actually benefiting many

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:34.804
people as we can see that the spread
of some of these ideals of democracy or

00:04:34.804 --> 00:04:42.380
the opportunities that are offered by an
increasingly integrated global economy and

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:46.520
an interdependent world has undoubtedly
lifted many people out of poverty.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.730
In fact, in the last few decades,

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:54.020
more people have been lifted out of
poverty than any time in human history.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:57.760
More people are going to school
than any time in human history.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:02.000
More people have access to clean
drinking water or sanitation.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.915
And yes, it's also true that
more women are actually

00:05:05.915 --> 00:05:09.492
participating in the global
economy than ever.

00:05:09.492 --> 00:05:13.682
In fact, the original goals that we
set in 2000 for the global agenda,

00:05:13.682 --> 00:05:16.743
which were called
the Millennial Development Goals,

00:05:16.743 --> 00:05:20.733
had as a simple objective to halve
the number of people living in poverty

00:05:20.733 --> 00:05:24.350
over the 15-year period
from the year 2000 to 2015.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.400
And in fact, we have achieved that.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.870
We have achieved that, and
we should be proud about that.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:34.190
This is a healthier and
wealthier world than at any time.

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:35.420
And but

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:40.460
at the same time, I have to take a step
back a little bit after these 15 years.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:45.964
And it reminds me a little bit of Dickens
who wrote in the 1870s this book,

00:05:45.964 --> 00:05:48.030
The Tale of Two Cities.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:50.975
At that time,
he was talking about Paris and London.

00:05:50.975 --> 00:05:53.300
I'm spending most of my time in London.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:57.155
But his book starts with, it was the best
of times, it was the worst of times, and

00:05:57.155 --> 00:05:59.849
I think we're a little bit
in that situation right now.

00:05:59.849 --> 00:06:03.628
We've lifted enormous
people out of poverty,

00:06:03.628 --> 00:06:07.883
we also have still challenges
that need to be solved.

00:06:07.883 --> 00:06:10.359
And many of these challenges, actually,

00:06:10.359 --> 00:06:14.710
I think we became fully aware of
that in the crisis in 2007-2008.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:18.799
Some people said it's a financial crisis,
and some people said it's a governmental

00:06:18.799 --> 00:06:22.910
crisis, then it became economic crisis,
and we're still recuperating from that.

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:27.940
In fact, it was the worst crisis on record
since the Great Depression in the 20s.

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:30.950
And I think it made us realize,
I've always found, by the way,

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:35.050
that this was more of a crisis
of morality than anything else.

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:38.870
But it made us realize that the system
we had created had certainly lifted so

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:42.680
many people out of poverty, but
it wasn't quite sustainable.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:50.230
It came at an enormous cost, a high
level of private or government debt.

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:55.586
An enormous level of overconsumption, and
frankly, leaving too many people behind.

00:06:55.586 --> 00:06:59.292
I've always said that in any system
where too many people feel that

00:06:59.292 --> 00:07:02.336
they're not participating or
are being left behind,

00:07:02.336 --> 00:07:06.724
that system will ultimately rebel against
itself just like a cancer in a body.

00:07:06.724 --> 00:07:10.156
And you've seen that happen increasingly,
so

00:07:10.156 --> 00:07:14.549
the question is have we learned
anything since this crisis?

00:07:14.549 --> 00:07:18.562
I hope the answer would be affirmative,
but if you look at the hot numbers,

00:07:18.562 --> 00:07:20.934
you have to really ask yourself questions.

00:07:20.934 --> 00:07:25.605
The debt that caused this crisis, by the
way in economic system that was very much

00:07:25.605 --> 00:07:29.998
around, we asked the Saudis to pump
the oil, we asked the Chinese to produce

00:07:29.998 --> 00:07:33.103
the plastic, and
we asked the Americans to consume.

00:07:33.103 --> 00:07:37.251
That's basically what we are doing
in a very simplistic way.

00:07:37.251 --> 00:07:42.487
And you'd think we would have learned form
that now that we are 8 years further or

00:07:42.487 --> 00:07:43.950
9 years further, but

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:48.049
the global debt actually has
gone up by another 57 trillion.

00:07:48.049 --> 00:07:50.498
That's 40% more global debt.

00:07:50.498 --> 00:07:56.824
Obviously, all these things have
played a role for it, basically,

00:07:56.824 --> 00:08:02.685
in Europe and the US Companies,
actually, you see the same behavior.

00:08:02.685 --> 00:08:06.660
In the future of companies, more being
given back on special dividends and

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:11.670
share buy-backs and the financial side,
because of this abundancy of capital.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:14.930
But very limited amounts of money
are entering in the real economy,

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:17.060
and you get over-consumption.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.820
I think we're still on this
linear consumption pass,

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:23.230
only 10% of the packaging,
for example, gets re-used.

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:27.630
90% ends up in landfills and
unfortunately more and more in the oceans.

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:29.320
That's not going to last either.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:32.560
So on the consumption pattern,
we still haven't moved to a circular

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:34.700
consumption pattern at
the speed that is required.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:38.510
In fact you take the top 1.2
billion people in this world

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:41.290
that to which we all belong,
believe it or not.

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:46.922
But the top 1.2 billion consume
75% of the worlds resources and

00:08:46.922 --> 00:08:49.842
the bottom 1 billion, only 1%.

00:08:49.842 --> 00:08:54.106
But I can assure you, if you are on the
bottom, you all aspire to live like us and

00:08:54.106 --> 00:08:56.430
the numbers don't add up anymore.

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:59.740
We're living way beyond the limits
of our planetary boundaries and

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:02.710
climate change obviously is one
of the effects that we see.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.080
And then you think we left
too many people behind.

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:08.620
Well, we're again a little bit further and
frankly in the next,

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.010
the [INAUDIBLE] since the crisis,

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:13.590
the Gini Coefficient, in all countries
in the world has actually gone up.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:16.570
The only country that is an exception
I believe is Ecuador, by memory, but

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:18.970
the rest,
we've actually increased the gap.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:23.702
And now it's 62 people in this world
that have the same net wealth as

00:09:23.702 --> 00:09:25.100
the bottom 95%.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:30.078
In fact, the top 1% in the world has

00:09:30.078 --> 00:09:35.224
the same wealth as the bottom 99%.

00:09:35.224 --> 00:09:40.074
We've created, really,
a system that is not sustainable,

00:09:40.074 --> 00:09:43.960
but we've certainly not made it better.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:48.450
And one of the key effects that you
see from this is climate change.

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:53.367
In fact, the World Bank's Jim Kim
estimates that we have just passed the 1

00:09:53.367 --> 00:09:58.052
degree towards the 2 degrees which
is about the maximum that everybody

00:09:58.052 --> 00:10:02.525
thinks this world can handle before
the consequences become dire.

00:10:02.525 --> 00:10:05.990
Lots of people are starting to
talk now about 1.5 degrees.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:09.862
In fact, scientists are finding out,
it's the bleeding of the coral reefs for

00:10:09.862 --> 00:10:13.440
example,above 1.5 degrees,
the coral reefs are gone.

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:16.730
Scientifically now,
basically well established.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:17.580
And if that happens,

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.220
you'd put the likelihood of about
a billion people at risk again.

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:25.330
But interestingly, Jim Kim from
the World Bank issued a report fairly

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:28.140
recently that we stay on
the current trajectory

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:31.100
then about 100 million more
people will fall into poverty.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:35.120
Climate change, as I've said many times,
is actually a human development agenda,

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.120
it's the poor that suffer.

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:38.780
When you add Hurricane Sandy or

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:42.180
other things in the US,
I have sons living in New York.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:43.720
They had a great time climbing the stairs.

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:45.860
They didn't have to go to the gym.

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:49.200
They saw some people a little bit more
than they were watching television.

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:50.090
But in the end of the day,

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:52.980
it was in a few weeks you were back
with the resilience you built up but

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:57.070
the people in Haiti and others,
they were pushed back another few years.

00:10:57.070 --> 00:11:03.210
Climate change Is a development agenda
issue, more than anything else.

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:06.150
14 of the last 15 years have
been the warmest on record.

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:08.980
So California has not
been excluded from this.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:13.030
I think Jerry Brown has been a fairly
outspoken governor in this state.

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:15.150
And the water shortages that you have, and

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:20.100
the extensions of the limitations of water
you can use as a good example of that.

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:23.870
In fact the IMF estimates that the real
cost of climate change to the global

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:27.718
economy, already today if you include
everything, which is air pollution,

00:11:27.718 --> 00:11:33.840
in China 1.8 million people a year
lose their lives to air pollution.

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:37.910
If you count all the externalities in
there, they're estimating that there is

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:44.000
about $5.3 trillion already now
in costs of actually not acting.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:47.090
Whereas just looking at
the Loman school of economics,

00:11:47.090 --> 00:11:51.100
I gather one of your competitors to some
extent, that said that climate change,

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:55.390
actually the economic losses
could go to about $27 trillion,

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:59.810
that’s about 20% of the world’s assets,
that’s quite a lot.

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:02.540
And the sustainable accounting
standard boards have in fact,

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:04.310
if you look in the US,

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:09.450
93% of the US equities would actually
be at risk because of climate change.

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:13.702
And it’s not just
the financial side of that,

00:12:13.702 --> 00:12:18.826
WWF was calculating that in the last,
since the 70s we

00:12:18.826 --> 00:12:24.623
have lost about 50% of our mammals,
reptiles, our birds.

00:12:24.623 --> 00:12:28.416
So it's a question a little bit of,
when is it our turn, in my opinion,

00:12:28.416 --> 00:12:31.980
if we don't take any actions without
being a doom state scenario.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:38.309
I was reading a book from who
Hubert Ribs was a Canadian philosopher.

00:12:38.309 --> 00:12:41.870
And Hubert Reeves said something
that stuck to me when I read this.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:45.080
He said, man is the most insane species.

00:12:46.330 --> 00:12:50.670
He worships an invisible God and
destroys a feasible nature,

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:56.779
not realizing that the feasible nature he
destroys is the invisible God he worships.

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:00.210
And that is about what we
are doing right now, and

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:03.710
that's obviously not a path
rate that is sustainable.

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:08.330
So I was very glad to see 2015
hopefully being a watershed year,

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:11.200
because in 2015,
two historic things happened.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:12.350
First of all,

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:16.230
the signing of the Sustainable
Development Goals in September in the UN.

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:21.050
With all the followup from the millennial
development goals covering the next

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:26.120
15 years, where 193 countries in
the world went together and said for

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:31.660
the next 15 years we have an opportunity
to irreversibly eradicate poverty.

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:35.519
Irreversibly eradicate poverty in
a more sustainable and equitable way.

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.850
Out of that came 17 goals.

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:41.500
Goal number 1, obviously,
poverty alleviation.

00:13:41.500 --> 00:13:43.090
Goal number 2, food security.

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:45.480
Goal number 5, gender equality.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:47.420
Goal number 6, water sanitation.

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:50.050
Goal number 16, governments.

00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:51.699
Goal number 17, partnerships.

00:13:51.699 --> 00:13:55.369
16 goals, that if we would work all
these goals, we could indeed do that.

00:13:56.560 --> 00:14:00.160
And the first test of these sustainable
development goals came in December,

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:01.740
last year in Paris.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:04.353
It was the famous Cup 21.

00:14:04.353 --> 00:14:09.114
The Cup 21 where all the countries in the
world began, about 190 countries submitted

00:14:09.114 --> 00:14:12.290
their intended nationally
determined commitments.

00:14:12.290 --> 00:14:17.720
What they thought they could do to
stem the tide of climate change.

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:22.400
We were on a four degree trajectory, and
lo and behold in Paris with the agreement.

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:24.580
We've actually bent that curve.

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:29.310
The agreement covers all countries, four
times more than the Kyoto Protocol, and

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:32.100
it was on a voluntary basis, you propose.

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:34.310
Just adding up what was proposed.

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:39.310
No back sliding, cannot go back, five year
review, $100 billion a year financing

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:42.860
to move forward obviously and
help the poor countries.

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.090
A pretty good agreement
as a starting point.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:50.300
Obviously we have discovered that we
cannot wait till the end of the century to

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:54.080
tackle climate change and
now we have to work on accelerating it.

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:55.810
But the base has been late.

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:57.660
So I'm actually very pleased that, for

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:02.350
the first time in history, we could
be the generation, if you want to.

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:07.260
And especially the young people here,
that solve poverty.

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:12.400
Which is a noble goal to go for by itself,
but also ensure that future generations

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:16.100
don't have to deal anymore with
the effects of climate change.

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:19.580
I was very pleased a few weeks
ago in New York where I was

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:23.648
present when 175 member state countries
actually rectified the Paris agreement.

00:15:23.648 --> 00:15:30.510
We need 55% of the countries, 55% of the
carbon emission to make that go into law.

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:35.860
But interesting to see the US right now,
the Chinese and some others are actually

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:41.980
trying to push this agenda forward to
ensure a more rapid implementation.

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:47.356
Now this agenda which is
an enormous agenda for

00:15:47.356 --> 00:15:52.212
humanity, zero property,
zero carbon emission.

00:15:52.212 --> 00:15:56.047
I don't think any business person
would set such an audacious goal for

00:15:56.047 --> 00:15:57.089
his own business.

00:15:57.089 --> 00:16:01.277
And certainly not if you tell him or
her that's in the next 15 years more or

00:16:01.277 --> 00:16:03.350
less That the thing needs to happen.

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:06.165
So we won't get there if we
don't work all together, and

00:16:06.165 --> 00:16:08.790
business has an enormous role to play.

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:11.340
Which is the only thing really
I want to leave you with,

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:16.650
that more businesses need to start
taking a higher level of responsibility

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:18.640
to be part of that solution.

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:21.900
After all, there is no business
case in enduring poverty.

00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:26.440
I've discover that a long time ago,
even in our company which is fortunate

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:30.370
enough to be in 190 countries or
reach 2 billion consumers a day.

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:34.880
We have to be sure that this
is a more inclusive society.

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:37.720
An interesting statistic,
not to bother you with that, but

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:41.690
the world spends 9.7 trillion
a year on violence and

00:16:41.690 --> 00:16:47.214
war preventions and spendings on actually
having these things 9.7 trillion.

00:16:47.214 --> 00:16:51.680
If we would just take that money,
which is three times higher

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:53.400
than the development that
gentlemen I am talking about.

00:16:54.690 --> 00:16:57.540
We could wipe out all the debt in
the emerging markets with that.

00:16:58.540 --> 00:17:03.390
And we could give some of the money
still that is left to Europe to

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:08.230
start stabilizing the European
monetary system like we're longer to.

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:11.970
This is just a little free from my home
base so that we get a little bit for

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:14.750
this, but
this is what we are currently doing.

00:17:14.750 --> 00:17:17.590
This world has become reactive and

00:17:17.590 --> 00:17:23.039
spends behind the symptoms of the results
instead of attacking the cause.

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:28.270
You see the same in other areas by the
way, healthcare's a good example where you

00:17:28.270 --> 00:17:33.770
have an enormous epidemic coming
up of diabetes two with obesity.

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:36.770
And we end up spending all
the money treating it instead of

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:37.960
going to prevention.

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:40.900
This is very much what we're doing
in the global economy as well.

00:17:40.900 --> 00:17:44.579
Now you won't solve this development
agenda if you don't get business involved.

00:17:44.579 --> 00:17:47.545
It's very simple,
the development agenda as I mentioned,

00:17:47.545 --> 00:17:50.627
cost about two to $3 trillion
a year which by the way is a pimple

00:17:50.627 --> 00:17:53.330
on the global economy of
100 to $110 trillion.

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:59.010
It's actually a shame that we have to talk
and knock on people's doors and, as we

00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:02.419
know, there's enough money that is going
around just have to put it to better use.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:07.530
But the overseas development aid from
all the governments in the world

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:10.899
has come down now to about $160 billion.

00:18:10.899 --> 00:18:13.440
And the pressure is more
downwards than upwards with all

00:18:14.440 --> 00:18:17.940
these attempts to reduce
these government deficits.

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:21.540
So you need to use that
relatively small amount of money

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:26.040
to unlock the private sector financing to
make this development agenda come alive.

00:18:27.250 --> 00:18:31.900
Now another statistic that would show you
why the private sector should be enforced

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:36.210
is because if you look in basically
the emerging markets which will be 80% of

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:42.400
the world population very soon, you'll
have the private sector be 60% of the GDP.

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:46.020
About 80% of the financial flow and
90% of the job creation.

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:48.190
So if you don't involve
the private sector,

00:18:48.190 --> 00:18:52.450
it's very hard to see how you can
actually then solve these things.

00:18:52.450 --> 00:18:56.658
And then the bigger companies have to
be obviously even more so involved.

00:18:56.658 --> 00:19:02.439
The top thousand companies in this world
have about 50% of the world market cap.

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:08.440
And not only that, they actually
have done, in terms of revenue,

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:11.846
you're talking about $35 trillion.

00:19:11.846 --> 00:19:14.920
So if you don't get these
big companies involved,

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:19.250
you don't get to that tipping point,
and we're obviously one of those.

00:19:19.250 --> 00:19:23.300
So yes, there is a self-interest to
make these economies,function, but

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:26.170
it is also enormous opportunity for
business,

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:29.330
obviously if they understand this and
be part of this.

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:30.800
Not being part of it,

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:33.910
I think risks the very essence of your
business model in the first place.

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:38.000
Now business has many responsibilities.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:41.730
I always like to quote Viktor Frankl who
wrote his book Man's Search For Meaning.

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:45.214
Viktor Frankl was
a survivor of Nazi Germany.

00:19:45.214 --> 00:19:49.930
And in his book, he wrote that when they

00:19:49.930 --> 00:19:53.210
built the Statue of Liberty on
the east coast of the United States,

00:19:53.210 --> 00:19:57.940
they forgot to build the statue of
responsibility on the west coast.

00:19:57.940 --> 00:20:01.810
There is no liberty
without responsibility.

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:06.430
So if you are a company of our size, or if
you are a country like the US or others,

00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:11.160
then you need to have that responsibility
to actively be part of that solution.

00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:15.080
For business it obviously means
responsibilities towards human rights,

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:19.000
towards paying our fair tax in
the places that we operate,

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:23.520
towards being part of fighting this
enormous disease of corruption

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:26.540
that is still everywhere in
many places in the world.

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:30.870
And I think an increasing amount of
businesses start to understand that,

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:33.410
enough for sure not, but
an increasing amount is.

00:20:33.410 --> 00:20:37.110
Business starts to
understand also increasingly

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:40.570
that not acting might actually
have a higher cost than acting.

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:42.880
I think we're actually
at that point right now,

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:45.309
that the cost of inaction is
higher than the cost of action.

00:20:46.630 --> 00:20:50.110
And you see that again if you look at
some of these numbers of climate change.

00:20:50.110 --> 00:20:54.350
But you look at food security, this is
still a world where about 800 million

00:20:54.350 --> 00:20:58.720
people go to bed hungry every night not
even knowing if they wake up the next day.

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:04.380
It's totally unexplainable if
you don't at the same time

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:08.430
realize that we're wasting 30 to 40%
of the food that we are producing.

00:21:08.430 --> 00:21:16.250
As if we don't care, the food that we
waste is about $800 billion every year.

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:17.690
Forget the effects on climate change and

00:21:17.690 --> 00:21:21.180
all the other things that's just
a small detail that comes with it.

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:23.670
But we're wasting $800 billion in food

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:27.339
while it actually only takes 80 billion to
feed these people that go to bed hungry.

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:31.390
Isn't that an equation that
humanity should be able to solve?

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:36.250
And yet, we are unable on the G7 or the
G20 to get food security on the agenda.

00:21:36.250 --> 00:21:40.430
That's, in fact,
as far as I'm concerned, a scandal.

00:21:40.430 --> 00:21:46.070
Now, equally there are enormous
opportunities in this development agenda.

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:49.890
Opportunities for business for
all businesses, if they really understand

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:54.770
that, just investing equally in this
world and women as we do in men.

00:21:54.770 --> 00:21:57.950
Access to financing,
education, land price,

00:21:57.950 --> 00:22:02.170
which unfortunately is not the case
in the majority of the country still,

00:22:02.170 --> 00:22:05.337
would unlocked the global
economy by $28 trillion.

00:22:06.530 --> 00:22:10.891
Any one dollar in nutrition,
we still have a hundred 60 million

00:22:10.891 --> 00:22:15.520
stunted children being born every year and
it could be your child or mine.

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:20.140
Just investing $1 gives
a $17 back to society.

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:24.930
Investing $1 in water and
sanitation has a payback of $6.

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:29.750
Any business would love to have
these returns on their investments.

00:22:29.750 --> 00:22:31.750
And yet, why don't we do that.

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:34.280
If we have such enormous opportunity's

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:37.200
why can we not get the world together and
do this.

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:41.670
I think the first main element
that is clearly missing is trust.

00:22:41.670 --> 00:22:43.550
It's an easy word, trust.

00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:47.490
What we are running currently is a society
where everybody mistrust's each other.

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:51.880
If you look at the Edelman survey which
is published every year at the time of

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:55.310
the World Economic Forum, you will
see that trust in governments is at

00:22:55.310 --> 00:22:59.370
an all-time low, trust in business,
trust in CEOs even lower.

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:03.090
And as a result of that,
obviously many people have given up.

00:23:03.090 --> 00:23:07.460
I think one of the extremes that you see
this year in your political cycle in this

00:23:07.460 --> 00:23:11.580
country, which is a great amusement
to all of us living abroad.

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:12.725
And I'm glad I live abroad.

00:23:12.725 --> 00:23:16.000
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Watching it from a distance, but

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:18.620
it is really, people are fed up.

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:23.320
They've all become against anything
because the established order is clearly

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:27.650
not attacking the issues that need
to be attacked with a maturity and

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:29.680
sense of morality that is needed.

00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:32.070
And that is the same by the way
in many other countries.

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:37.530
It just kept, doesn't get as much share
of voice, as obviously the US media does.

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:41.120
But trust is very low and
trust in business is even lower as I said.

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:44.590
And that's not surprising, because we've
seen an enormous amounts of scandals.

00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:47.530
And normally I could take
the Financial Times of the day and

00:23:47.530 --> 00:23:51.490
I can open everyday of Financial Times,
and as one or another company that gets

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:55.391
caught because they refuse to invest in
safety and the well and the goals for.

00:23:55.391 --> 00:23:59.407
They refuse to invest in
the safety of the workers and

00:23:59.407 --> 00:24:02.241
a factory collapses in Bangladesh.

00:24:02.241 --> 00:24:05.158
Or they manipulate LIBOR or
foreign exchange rates, or

00:24:05.158 --> 00:24:09.160
they start to tinker with the emissions
standards of their engines.

00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:12.120
And the list goes on, the list goes on.

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:15.570
And in this age of transparency,
what companies need to understand is

00:24:15.570 --> 00:24:18.480
that there really isn't
any place to hide anymore.

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:21.820
I don't think that these issues are more
or less than they were before, but

00:24:21.820 --> 00:24:24.170
they are certainly more
transparent than they were before.

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:24.930
That I can tell you.

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:30.590
So companies need to take notice of that
and be sure that the way they operate is

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:36.900
transparent, and take responsibility for
that if we want to build that trust.

00:24:36.900 --> 00:24:39.620
Trust can only come from transparency.

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:43.580
Transparency builds trust and
trust is needed to build prosperity.

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:47.750
And any time these companies don't
behave responsibly, you see over and

00:24:47.750 --> 00:24:51.690
over that about 30 to 40% of
their market kept disappears.

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:55.470
So increasingly, not surprisingly it's
interesting also to the financial market

00:24:55.470 --> 00:25:01.640
that want to know that what they
are investing is responsible investing.

00:25:01.640 --> 00:25:05.111
The other thing we need is
obviously transparency, as I said,

00:25:05.111 --> 00:25:06.363
that goes with trust.

00:25:06.363 --> 00:25:09.280
I don't think you can build
trust without that transparency.

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:12.411
We are publishing far more data now
as a company than we've ever done in

00:25:12.411 --> 00:25:15.950
the history of mankind, I believe,
and we will continue to do that.

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:20.760
Because by showing what we do, by giving
access to many people, we can build that.

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:23.110
The other thing that is needed
is a different business model.

00:25:23.110 --> 00:25:27.030
If you want to solve all these issues
like climate change, or food security, or

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:30.460
poverty alleviation, or access to
clean drinking water, or education,

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:34.150
if you want to, you cannot be
victim to the quarterly reporting.

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:39.029
This market and this wealth has become
incredibly short term, incredibly myopic,

00:25:39.029 --> 00:25:43.841
and many companies, unfortunately, would
forego doing the right decisions long

00:25:43.841 --> 00:25:47.843
term if it affects their quarterly
profits versus market guidance.

00:25:47.843 --> 00:25:49.514
It's become very myopic.

00:25:49.514 --> 00:25:54.211
And not surprisingly, the average length
of a publicly traded company in the US now

00:25:54.211 --> 00:25:55.833
has come down to 17 years.

00:25:55.833 --> 00:26:00.570
The average tenure of
a CEO is now 4.5 years.

00:26:00.570 --> 00:26:05.420
In that short cycle, these longer term
issues that really are important for

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:08.140
all of us are not getting
the attention that they need.

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:09.610
So you need different business models,

00:26:09.610 --> 00:26:13.030
and even when I became CEO we
stopped doing quarterly reporting.

00:26:13.030 --> 00:26:14.280
We stopped giving guidance.

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:17.540
We moved our compensation
systems to the long term.

00:26:17.540 --> 00:26:21.530
But more importantly,
we developed business models that

00:26:21.530 --> 00:26:25.540
really are firmly ingrained in
the principle of partnerships.

00:26:25.540 --> 00:26:29.810
It was Einstein who said the definition of
insanity is to do the same thing over and

00:26:29.810 --> 00:26:31.900
over again and expect different results.

00:26:31.900 --> 00:26:33.800
That's not going to work anymore.

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:37.620
We've come so far, if we now really
want to snap out of this and

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:40.780
create this better wealth for
everybody, we have to become adults and

00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:42.800
start working at
a different level as well.

00:26:42.800 --> 00:26:45.990
And that different level has to
come via trust, as I mentioned, but

00:26:45.990 --> 00:26:48.900
certainly a different form
of partnership as well.

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:50.810
It's a partnership more than just you and

00:26:50.810 --> 00:26:55.310
I working together because the two of us
can do more than each of us individually.

00:26:55.310 --> 00:26:58.250
It's first and foremost a partnership for
the common good.

00:26:59.410 --> 00:27:04.260
Where we put the interests of others ahead
of our own, knowing that by doing so

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:06.860
we will actually be better
off in the longer term.

00:27:08.130 --> 00:27:12.590
If we get that higher level of
partnership, of moral morality,

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:16.260
if you want to, I think we can
solve any of the world's problems.

00:27:16.260 --> 00:27:22.470
We don't need more PhDs, in due respect,
or missions to Mars to find the answer.

00:27:22.470 --> 00:27:26.420
For most of these issues of the
development agenda, we have the answer.

00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:32.820
And frankly, increasingly we
see others becoming aware of

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:36.690
the enormous potentials or
the downside of risks if we don't act.

00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:41.480
I personally would only want to invest
in companies that create a better future

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:44.320
where I can retire in or
my children can live in.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:46.970
So we see people becoming
much more conscious

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:49.545
of how they use the power of their wallet.

00:27:49.545 --> 00:27:52.610
Millennials are the best example of that.

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:56.375
In fact, this is the first year that the
millennials in the US will have the same

00:27:56.375 --> 00:27:59.528
number of voting power
as the baby boomers.

00:27:59.528 --> 00:28:02.660
Unfortunately, they're not
as politically engaged

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:05.767
because they've been turned off
a little bit by what is happening.

00:28:05.767 --> 00:28:07.621
But it's the same now.

00:28:07.621 --> 00:28:09.644
In 2020, which is not that far off,

00:28:09.644 --> 00:28:12.572
we have significantly more
millennials as voting power.

00:28:12.572 --> 00:28:15.965
And these people are far
more purpose driven,

00:28:15.965 --> 00:28:20.924
they are far more conscious of how
they put their dollars to use, and

00:28:20.924 --> 00:28:26.330
hopefully will be a big wave of
change that will benefit us all.

00:28:26.330 --> 00:28:28.630
But the financial evidence is also there.

00:28:28.630 --> 00:28:31.550
I personally don't believe that the thing
will move at the speed that we need

00:28:31.550 --> 00:28:34.450
to move it if we don't get
the financial market involved.

00:28:34.450 --> 00:28:36.220
Unfortunately, if you want to,

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:40.550
it is still a global economy that
very heavily depends on that.

00:28:40.550 --> 00:28:43.340
And the interesting thing is
that most of the studies now

00:28:43.340 --> 00:28:46.150
actually show that companies that
are more transparent obviously

00:28:46.150 --> 00:28:50.380
take risks away from the financial
market and have a lower cost of capital.

00:28:50.380 --> 00:28:53.470
It also shows that you get
better operational performance.

00:28:53.470 --> 00:28:55.773
Companies that think
about climate change or

00:28:55.773 --> 00:28:59.417
some if these issues also make their
whole value chain more resilient.

00:28:59.417 --> 00:29:05.710
And then not surprisingly, the stock price
performance, everything else being equal,

00:29:05.710 --> 00:29:11.202
is actually better off, companies that
are more responsible in this market.

00:29:11.202 --> 00:29:15.568
What Unilever has done with their Unilever
Sustainable Living Plan, that Jim briefly

00:29:15.568 --> 00:29:19.933
referred to, is actually create a business
model where we totally decouple our growth

00:29:19.933 --> 00:29:24.040
from environmental impact and
increase our overall social impact.

00:29:24.040 --> 00:29:27.850
But the difference from CSR and staying
in your own shop if you want to, or

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:32.000
making it only part of your company is
that we fully integrate this in all we do.

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:34.050
It is in fact our business model, and

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:36.765
we take responsibility of
the total value chain.

00:29:36.765 --> 00:29:40.880
You cannot solve issues if you just
stay in your own narrow little box and

00:29:40.880 --> 00:29:41.930
work inside it.

00:29:41.930 --> 00:29:44.530
If in our case, you're in the food
business, you have to take

00:29:44.530 --> 00:29:49.190
responsibility from the farm to the fork
and all the issues that are in between.

00:29:49.190 --> 00:29:53.200
The issue of malnutrition or stunting,
the issues of climate smart agriculture,

00:29:53.200 --> 00:29:58.640
the issues of food waste, the issues of
creating jobs for small holder farmers,

00:29:58.640 --> 00:30:02.940
the issue of deforestation, half of it
driven by the enormous demand of food.

00:30:02.940 --> 00:30:05.520
And on the other hand,
the issues of obesity and

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:07.510
some of the other things we talked about.

00:30:07.510 --> 00:30:11.010
Yes, if you want to be a responsible
company and you're in that business,

00:30:11.010 --> 00:30:15.000
you then also have to be
co-responsible to finding solutions

00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:18.440
to these issues that show up
in your total value chain.

00:30:18.440 --> 00:30:20.725
I think that the divestiture movement or

00:30:20.725 --> 00:30:25.590
the financial market is getting
increasingly interested in this agenda.

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:30.380
We had in Paris at the COP 21,
$24 trillion of capital and

00:30:30.380 --> 00:30:33.580
the management asking for
a price on carbon.

00:30:33.580 --> 00:30:36.536
We thought in Paris that we could
get the divestiture movement,

00:30:36.536 --> 00:30:37.750
the carbon divestiture.

00:30:37.750 --> 00:30:41.950
You see a lot of that at universities
now as your endowment funds, etc.

00:30:41.950 --> 00:30:45.050
We thought we could get to 100 billion.

00:30:45.050 --> 00:30:47.760
We actually got 900 billion
at the time of Paris, and

00:30:47.760 --> 00:30:50.640
that number has gone up
to 2.7 trillion now.

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:53.140
So the financial market is catching on.

00:30:53.140 --> 00:30:58.180
And you can see that 57 companies in
the US have gone bankrupt that were

00:30:58.180 --> 00:31:02.220
in the coal business, Peabody the last
one, just in the last two years alone.

00:31:02.220 --> 00:31:03.696
You can see it in other things.

00:31:03.696 --> 00:31:09.442
Tesla makes actually relatively few cars,
their Model 3, tremendously popular,

00:31:09.442 --> 00:31:14.420
325,000 orders for
that car in the first few days alone.

00:31:14.420 --> 00:31:18.510
Despite selling less than one-twenties or
one-thirties of the cars of

00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:23.120
General Motors, it has already half
the market cap of General Motors.

00:31:23.120 --> 00:31:29.590
Increasingly, the financial
community is folding with the and

00:31:29.590 --> 00:31:33.800
they're getting actively involved as
they see this as an enormous risk, but

00:31:33.800 --> 00:31:35.640
also an enormous opportunity.

00:31:35.640 --> 00:31:40.060
Investment in green energy right now
is far exceeding the investment in

00:31:40.060 --> 00:31:44.490
fossil fuel, even with the low
prices in fact that we have.

00:31:44.490 --> 00:31:46.769
Now let me pick up pace and do only one or

00:31:46.769 --> 00:31:49.560
two more things before we
open it up to Q and As.

00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:53.859
I think the first one is the millennials
because there are many millennials in

00:31:53.859 --> 00:31:54.917
the audience, and

00:31:54.917 --> 00:31:59.300
I would just say that a purpose-driven
business model makes a lot more sense.

00:31:59.300 --> 00:32:03.126
Only 30% of millennials actually
want to work for corporates.

00:32:03.126 --> 00:32:06.026
They just don't feel it's cool
anymore to work for corporates, for

00:32:06.026 --> 00:32:08.350
the reasons that we mentioned.

00:32:08.350 --> 00:32:12.100
Since we moved to this much more
stronger purpose doing business model.

00:32:12.100 --> 00:32:14.990
We actually find ourselves to
be the third most looked up

00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:17.520
company in LinkedIn after Google and
Apple.

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:19.660
Not even being a product name you know,

00:32:19.660 --> 00:32:24.140
Lever is sort of the holding name and
all of our products have different names.

00:32:24.140 --> 00:32:27.650
We had, at one point, 8 million people
applying to our company every year.

00:32:27.650 --> 00:32:31.000
And in most countries that we operate,
we're the preferred employers.

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:34.910
So even from that point of view, it makes
sense to be able to attract the best and

00:32:34.910 --> 00:32:35.870
brightest.

00:32:35.870 --> 00:32:39.220
Would certainly guarantee
a future of your company.

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:41.020
What we also see clearly is,

00:32:41.020 --> 00:32:44.950
that millennials don't want
to just be part of this.

00:32:44.950 --> 00:32:48.650
They very much understand that the next 15
years are going to be crucial for them.

00:32:48.650 --> 00:32:50.790
They want to be an active
part in shaping this future.

00:32:50.790 --> 00:32:54.430
I had the pressure to be serving
on the high level panel,

00:32:54.430 --> 00:32:57.800
to develop these sustainable development
goals for the secretary general.

00:32:57.800 --> 00:33:00.980
And we put this website out there,
The World We Want.

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:02.240
And within a nanosecond,

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:06.500
we had over a million young people, many
from Africa, writing down what they want.

00:33:06.500 --> 00:33:09.740
They want to be actively involved in
shaping this agenda and too often,

00:33:09.740 --> 00:33:12.880
we still exclude this young generation.

00:33:12.880 --> 00:33:16.550
Next to the millennials, we also obviously
need a different type of leadership.

00:33:16.550 --> 00:33:19.120
I was very excited to hear
that here at Stanford,

00:33:19.120 --> 00:33:23.760
you're actually trying to put this
curriculum together for a future leaders.

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:26.720
And that you have a first coming home

00:33:26.720 --> 00:33:29.530
with the young global leaders
from developed economic forum.

00:33:29.530 --> 00:33:32.110
That's a very exciting step
you are talking there, but

00:33:32.110 --> 00:33:35.700
it is very clear that the leaders of
tomorrow need to be purpose driven.

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:37.940
Need to be able to work in partnership.

00:33:37.940 --> 00:33:40.880
Need to feel comfortable with
that level of transparency.

00:33:40.880 --> 00:33:45.710
Need to be systemic thinkers to be
able to handle this complexity, but

00:33:45.710 --> 00:33:47.930
above all, need to be human beings.

00:33:47.930 --> 00:33:51.250
Human beings with a high level
of awareness and engagement and

00:33:51.250 --> 00:33:53.820
a certain level of humanity and humility.

00:33:53.820 --> 00:33:55.370
If we get more of these leaders,

00:33:55.370 --> 00:33:59.540
and we're clearly short of it now,
looking at the average tenure of a CEO.

00:33:59.540 --> 00:34:03.590
But if we get more of these leaders,
I think that we're on the right path

00:34:03.590 --> 00:34:06.960
to solve some of these
key issues that we have.

00:34:07.970 --> 00:34:14.060
So in conclusion, I think we are,
we should not feel despair.

00:34:14.060 --> 00:34:17.480
I think we're moving in the right
direction, but the challenge is obviously,

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:18.350
how fast we move.

00:34:18.350 --> 00:34:21.590
Because the longer we wait, the more
difficult the challenge will be or

00:34:21.590 --> 00:34:23.130
the more expensive it will be.

00:34:23.130 --> 00:34:24.780
But for me, the reason to move fast is,

00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:28.500
the longer we wait,
the more people will live in poverty.

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:30.690
And there's no excuse
that we let that happen.

00:34:30.690 --> 00:34:34.900
I've always felt that if you belong to the
2% of the world population that can do and

00:34:34.900 --> 00:34:36.080
like what they want.

00:34:36.080 --> 00:34:40.430
Go to wonderful institutions like this,
find a job they like or work.

00:34:40.430 --> 00:34:45.030
Don't have to worry about their child not
making the age of five or being stunted.

00:34:46.410 --> 00:34:49.570
Or being included in the workforce and
all the other things.

00:34:49.570 --> 00:34:51.260
If you belong to those 2%,

00:34:51.260 --> 00:34:55.170
then it's obviously your obligation
to work for the other 98%.

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:59.570
And that is what we're
trying to do in Unilever.

00:34:59.570 --> 00:35:02.260
It was Martin Luther King
that actually said,

00:35:02.260 --> 00:35:06.010
that everyone can be great
because everyone can serve.

00:35:06.010 --> 00:35:10.710
And all of us in our own humble
ways can make the difference to

00:35:10.710 --> 00:35:13.100
together create a better world for all.

00:35:13.100 --> 00:35:13.850
And certainly for

00:35:13.850 --> 00:35:17.440
generations to come and that's
obviously the world I want to live in.

00:35:17.440 --> 00:35:20.670
And I want to be part of
that challenge to say,

00:35:20.670 --> 00:35:24.120
this is what we really did
in the coming 15 years.

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:26.470
So that nobody else has to
worry about these challenges.

00:35:27.850 --> 00:35:32.290
Unlike the Dalai Lama,
one of the things the Dalai Lama said that

00:35:33.550 --> 00:35:37.060
If you seek enlightenment for yourself.

00:35:37.060 --> 00:35:42.570
Simply to enhance yourself and
your position, you miss purpose,

00:35:42.570 --> 00:35:46.530
but if you seek enlightenment for
yourself to enable to serve others.

00:35:46.530 --> 00:35:52.130
You are with purpose, so my message to
you very simply is, go with purpose.

00:35:52.130 --> 00:35:52.818
Thank you very much.

00:35:52.818 --> 00:35:58.442
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Well Paul, thank you so

00:35:58.442 --> 00:36:03.940
much for those inspiring words.

00:36:03.940 --> 00:36:08.794
I think you've left us with

00:36:08.794 --> 00:36:13.858
many things to think about,

00:36:13.858 --> 00:36:22.350
but there were some
exciting things you said.

00:36:22.350 --> 00:36:26.040
There are signs that governments,
companies,

00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:30.749
people in general are normally
more aware for taking action.

00:36:32.050 --> 00:36:35.920
To do something about the development
agenda about sustainability.

00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:40.790
But that was not really
the case a few years back

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:43.310
when you took over Unilever as CEO.

00:36:44.320 --> 00:36:48.610
And you came in first day and
made huge changes.

00:36:51.420 --> 00:36:54.810
How did you handle all of the challenges
and the resistance that came with that?

00:36:55.865 --> 00:36:59.150
&gt;&gt; Well, there were changes indeed,
but first of all,

00:36:59.150 --> 00:37:02.490
there was a burning platform and
in fact, I had two of them.

00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:08.210
I came in from the outside, which
helped a little by not being so biased.

00:37:08.210 --> 00:37:11.580
I thought some of the changes
that needed to be made perhaps.

00:37:11.580 --> 00:37:14.720
But the business was not doing so well, so

00:37:14.720 --> 00:37:18.250
that was a burning platform and
then you had the crisis in the 2007, 2008.

00:37:18.250 --> 00:37:18.940
And so,

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:24.380
it wasn't difficult to convince people
that we needed to do things differently.

00:37:24.380 --> 00:37:27.570
Because I came in from the outside and
actually,

00:37:27.570 --> 00:37:31.250
I had worked what some people
thought was competition in the past.

00:37:31.250 --> 00:37:32.710
I don't have that concept myself,

00:37:32.710 --> 00:37:37.860
but some people felt that the Trojan
horse was let into the company.

00:37:37.860 --> 00:37:40.520
And I discovered very
early on that it was for

00:37:40.520 --> 00:37:43.520
me to prove that I could
be part of the team.

00:37:43.520 --> 00:37:46.490
Instead of them to prove that
they could be part of my team.

00:37:46.490 --> 00:37:47.880
And I had to establish myself, so

00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:52.570
I spent an enormous amount of time
digging into the history of the company.

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:54.380
Jim Collins, in his book,
&gt;From Good to Great,

00:37:54.380 --> 00:37:58.940
talks about nurturing the core
before you stimulate progress.

00:37:58.940 --> 00:38:01.350
And we actually went back to
the roots of this company.

00:38:01.350 --> 00:38:02.780
We actually went to Port Sunlight,

00:38:02.780 --> 00:38:07.750
which is in the north of England
where the company had started.

00:38:07.750 --> 00:38:11.230
Symbolically had a meeting there
with our senior management and

00:38:11.230 --> 00:38:13.470
discussed a little what
made this company great.

00:38:13.470 --> 00:38:15.790
And we came to some enormous values and

00:38:15.790 --> 00:38:18.240
one of them is certainly
doing well by doing good.

00:38:18.240 --> 00:38:23.080
This was a man by the way that,
instituted in the UK the six

00:38:23.080 --> 00:38:28.060
hour day was very revolutionary
in the 1800s I can tell you.

00:38:28.060 --> 00:38:30.350
When he became a Lord and
he went into parliament,

00:38:30.350 --> 00:38:31.549
he introduced the pension plans.

00:38:33.810 --> 00:38:37.480
When his employees would go to war,
volunteer for

00:38:37.480 --> 00:38:40.460
the wars that would add a World War I and
World War II.

00:38:40.460 --> 00:38:42.820
He obviously dealt only with World War I,

00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:47.540
but guaranteed the wages to the wives so
that they could live in.

00:38:47.540 --> 00:38:50.060
So, he was next to ordinary
man in that sense.

00:38:50.060 --> 00:38:52.980
But always have to believe what
doing well by doing good funding.

00:38:52.980 --> 00:38:56.150
The marines he would ship off
sustainable fishery at a round

00:38:56.150 --> 00:38:57.980
table of sustainable development.

00:38:57.980 --> 00:39:01.030
So what we did is,
we took all that strength and said,

00:39:01.030 --> 00:39:05.180
can we not turn it into a business model
that is desperately needed right now?

00:39:05.180 --> 00:39:06.910
Where we link all of this, and

00:39:06.910 --> 00:39:13.120
see this as an increasingly, powerful
driver for future business growth.

00:39:13.120 --> 00:39:15.550
And what the surprise in all of this,
it's hard work.

00:39:15.550 --> 00:39:18.670
It's not that easy obviously, and
some people are always skeptical but

00:39:18.670 --> 00:39:20.910
you need them to make better plans.

00:39:20.910 --> 00:39:25.560
But people rally quite
quickly towards something,

00:39:25.560 --> 00:39:28.700
when it is a higher purpose and
want to be part of that.

00:39:29.948 --> 00:39:32.570
People want to make a difference,
they want to live as well in a little bit

00:39:32.570 --> 00:39:35.690
better place,
they want to touch people positively.

00:39:35.690 --> 00:39:37.860
And if you can work for a company that,

00:39:37.860 --> 00:39:42.340
where those values can come alive,
then people feel happier.

00:39:42.340 --> 00:39:45.040
And you've seen it in
our engagement scores,

00:39:45.040 --> 00:39:48.100
which were sort of in the bottom
side when we started.

00:39:48.100 --> 00:39:52.780
And now, they're off the map,
because people want to be part of this.

00:39:52.780 --> 00:39:55.149
&gt;&gt; On that note, can you tell us,

00:39:55.149 --> 00:40:01.267
when did you find purpose in promoting
sustainability as a business practice?

00:40:01.267 --> 00:40:02.745
Well, the question is always,

00:40:02.745 --> 00:40:06.100
why didn't our predecessors deal with
this as much as they should have.

00:40:06.100 --> 00:40:09.980
And the answer is,
really the issue wasn't so evident.

00:40:09.980 --> 00:40:12.200
Because we've lifted so
many people out of poverty,

00:40:12.200 --> 00:40:14.810
we really got the issues
of planetary boundaries.

00:40:14.810 --> 00:40:18.670
If you go back, even at the time that the
original millennial development goals were

00:40:18.670 --> 00:40:19.430
developed.

00:40:19.430 --> 00:40:21.970
The word sustainable
didn't show up there even.

00:40:21.970 --> 00:40:23.170
There were issues of HIV.

00:40:23.170 --> 00:40:25.750
There were issues in
the developing markets.

00:40:25.750 --> 00:40:27.770
Now many of these issues
have become global,

00:40:27.770 --> 00:40:29.720
because of our inter dependent wealth.

00:40:29.720 --> 00:40:33.610
So, it's only in the last 10 years
that people have become fully aware of

00:40:33.610 --> 00:40:35.480
what needed to be changed.

00:40:35.480 --> 00:40:41.310
That's why you see this momentum now,
but I would not worry about that.

00:40:41.310 --> 00:40:46.270
We now have to do is to be sure that
we can accelerate, because as usual,

00:40:46.270 --> 00:40:49.550
there's always a little bit of vested
order that doesn't like to change.

00:40:49.550 --> 00:40:53.070
If you're on the right side of the
equation in this transformation of this

00:40:53.070 --> 00:40:54.250
global economy.

00:40:54.250 --> 00:40:56.130
You are more likely to be resistant,

00:40:56.130 --> 00:41:00.540
than if you are not cutting
the benefits on the global economy.

00:41:00.540 --> 00:41:04.710
But the reality right now is, about 99% of
the people are not getting the benefits of

00:41:04.710 --> 00:41:06.340
the global economy anymore.

00:41:06.340 --> 00:41:10.320
So, I hope we can build this momentum and
accelerate that to make the changes.

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:13.630
By the way, nothing to do with
being Democrat or Republican or

00:41:13.630 --> 00:41:16.550
being a Dutchman often
accused of smoking pot.

00:41:16.550 --> 00:41:19.990
And all the other thing,s [LAUGH]
which is liberalized in our country.

00:41:19.990 --> 00:41:24.970
And it just has to do with a common
sense agenda to make this world work for

00:41:24.970 --> 00:41:26.440
all of us long term.

00:41:26.440 --> 00:41:31.480
If you see the cost that Europe now has
on refugees, for example, it's enormous.

00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:34.050
And then,
you see the money that we spent is

00:41:34.050 --> 00:41:36.400
a pittance to actually solve these issues.

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:38.990
So it's becoming increasingly transparent,
I think,

00:41:38.990 --> 00:41:40.790
that this is an agenda for humanity.

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:46.260
&gt;&gt; The way you frame things to me,
it sounds like today,

00:41:46.260 --> 00:41:47.500
there's also a momentum.

00:41:47.500 --> 00:41:50.290
And today,
we are still at an inflection point

00:41:50.290 --> 00:41:53.400
companies could use to
change business practices.

00:41:53.400 --> 00:42:00.120
And yet, you don't see those that do
stand out, because not many are doing it.

00:42:00.120 --> 00:42:00.760
What is that?

00:42:00.760 --> 00:42:01.980
&gt;&gt; And that's a good question.

00:42:01.980 --> 00:42:03.100
That's a good question.

00:42:03.100 --> 00:42:05.730
I start from the premise that I
believe in the goodness of people.

00:42:05.730 --> 00:42:09.440
Because I never met a CEO who wants more
unemployment or more climate change or

00:42:09.440 --> 00:42:11.030
more pollution.

00:42:11.030 --> 00:42:15.330
Unless they don't tell me, but
I don't think they are there really, so

00:42:15.330 --> 00:42:17.580
you have to believe in
the goodness of all of us.

00:42:17.580 --> 00:42:19.420
And then, you have to ask
yourself the question, well,

00:42:19.420 --> 00:42:21.380
if that is the case,
why doesn't it happen?

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:23.640
And there are obviously many reasons for
that.

00:42:23.640 --> 00:42:25.220
The global economy isn't functioning,

00:42:25.220 --> 00:42:29.440
so you have to work very hard to
even keep your own company afloat.

00:42:29.440 --> 00:42:32.120
These issues of tenure,
that you might not have the skill sets, so

00:42:32.120 --> 00:42:33.840
there are many of those reasons.

00:42:33.840 --> 00:42:38.720
But I think in essence, it boils down to,
the behavior of people ss

00:42:38.720 --> 00:42:44.260
governed by the boundaries in which you
operate and that is true for all of us.

00:42:44.260 --> 00:42:47.220
If you have become a father or
a mother and you have children.

00:42:47.220 --> 00:42:50.910
You start to think of different boundaries
and you have different behaviors.

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:53.680
You might not go out to the bar every
night anymore with your buddies.

00:42:53.680 --> 00:42:56.010
You might want to do a little
bit more babysitting or

00:42:56.010 --> 00:42:57.630
sit together with your kids.

00:42:57.630 --> 00:43:01.600
If I tell a salesman that he gets rewarded
on the number of orders he writes.

00:43:01.600 --> 00:43:05.030
Sales orders, he's going to split many
orders and write a lot of orders.

00:43:05.030 --> 00:43:08.970
If I tell him he's going to be rewarded
on the total sales volume he brings in,

00:43:08.970 --> 00:43:10.080
he might have a different behavior.

00:43:10.080 --> 00:43:13.630
And there are three main things in this
world that drive people's behavior in

00:43:13.630 --> 00:43:15.070
a dysfunctional way.

00:43:15.070 --> 00:43:17.060
The first one is the short
term versus long term, so

00:43:17.060 --> 00:43:20.740
we have to move the market
to the longer term.

00:43:20.740 --> 00:43:23.900
Larry Fink who runs the BlackRock,
one of the biggest institutions.

00:43:23.900 --> 00:43:27.920
Has written about that, because everybody
sees this destructiveness of that.

00:43:27.920 --> 00:43:31.370
The second thing is this
balance between man and nature.

00:43:31.370 --> 00:43:32.830
We have put the boundaries wrong.

00:43:32.830 --> 00:43:35.970
If you again and again,
not that I want to be religious here,

00:43:35.970 --> 00:43:39.740
but if you go to the book of Genesis,
it says mans dominance over nature.

00:43:39.740 --> 00:43:43.810
And we have started to interpret that a,s
that we can literally dominate nature and

00:43:43.810 --> 00:43:44.490
destroy nature.

00:43:44.490 --> 00:43:49.250
That we are smarter, but our intelligence
was meant to be stewards of nature.

00:43:49.250 --> 00:43:52.310
So we have to give nature
a value in all we do and

00:43:52.310 --> 00:43:55.170
we need to move the narrow
definition of GDP.

00:43:55.170 --> 00:44:00.720
Or the profit and loss statement in
a company to more integrated reporting.

00:44:00.720 --> 00:44:07.420
Including, the environmental and social
capital next to the financial capital.

00:44:07.420 --> 00:44:10.400
Capitalists are very good
at optimizing capital, but

00:44:10.400 --> 00:44:13.630
we've only put them on the paths
to optimize financial capital.

00:44:13.630 --> 00:44:15.070
But if we put a price on carbon,

00:44:15.070 --> 00:44:20.080
for example, we would move much faster
in bringing climate change down.

00:44:20.080 --> 00:44:23.620
Just like you put a price on the water,
we bring water uses down.

00:44:23.620 --> 00:44:28.170
So move to environmental and
social accounting, next to financial,

00:44:28.170 --> 00:44:29.840
is absolutely a must.

00:44:29.840 --> 00:44:34.410
And then the third element of the
boundaries is really our financial system.

00:44:34.410 --> 00:44:40.640
Our financial system's rewards are all on
capital and unfortunately, not on labor.

00:44:40.640 --> 00:44:43.200
Because they were designed
at a different time, but

00:44:43.200 --> 00:44:45.140
we have been slow at evolving it.

00:44:46.340 --> 00:44:48.630
One of the reasons that we've
been slow at evolving is,

00:44:48.630 --> 00:44:53.050
that most of the institutions that we
have created at the time of Breton Woods.

00:44:53.050 --> 00:44:57.080
Which was a long time ago for
you, in the 40s, but

00:44:57.080 --> 00:45:02.220
it was when 90% or 85% of the world
economy was in Europe and the US.

00:45:02.220 --> 00:45:07.430
Now, we have a global economy and
it's interdependent and

00:45:07.430 --> 00:45:10.970
we haven't figured out yet,
how to create the institutions to rule it.

00:45:10.970 --> 00:45:14.650
And we need to move to a system
where we don't reward capital.

00:45:14.650 --> 00:45:16.750
You take this country,
I was in New York on Monday and

00:45:16.750 --> 00:45:20.840
Tuesday, a few hedge funds guys,
and I'm not against them.

00:45:20.840 --> 00:45:24.870
But a few hedge funds guys last
year together made ten billion.

00:45:24.870 --> 00:45:26.320
That tax rate probably is 10%.

00:45:26.320 --> 00:45:30.980
Then they give 25 million to charity
to help the homeless in New York and

00:45:30.980 --> 00:45:31.640
they're heroes.

00:45:31.640 --> 00:45:37.060
They're all of a sudden
philanthropists of the highest order.

00:45:37.060 --> 00:45:41.790
they probably use the 25 million to
offset their taxable on the 10%,

00:45:41.790 --> 00:45:44.410
and but they created
the problem in the first place.

00:45:44.410 --> 00:45:49.580
Because our system,
we watch the capital at no tax and

00:45:49.580 --> 00:45:53.110
the people that work and
labor often catch 40, 50% tax.

00:45:53.110 --> 00:45:57.510
At the time that we need to create more
jobs, we need to subsidize labor and

00:45:57.510 --> 00:46:01.010
penalize capital,
because we have enough of the capital.

00:46:01.010 --> 00:46:02.570
We don't need to get higher returns.

00:46:02.570 --> 00:46:06.600
Now, I think that’s very radical thought
for some people in the U.S., but

00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:09.720
it’s absolutely needed if we want
to change the global economy.

00:46:09.720 --> 00:46:11.610
&gt;&gt; My gosh.

00:46:11.610 --> 00:46:15.130
After that answer, I have so
many questions about what you think of tax

00:46:15.130 --> 00:46:21.230
systems, supply chains,
governments, world thought leaders.

00:46:21.230 --> 00:46:28.340
But I'm going to take a pause right now
and allow the audience to make a question.

00:46:28.340 --> 00:46:31.470
There is going to be
a microphone over here and

00:46:31.470 --> 00:46:33.850
do we have a microphone
in the back as well?

00:46:33.850 --> 00:46:34.720
Okay.

00:46:34.720 --> 00:46:37.240
So we have a question right here.

00:46:37.240 --> 00:46:43.030
If you can just stand up, say your name,
and ask one question please.

00:46:44.160 --> 00:46:45.500
&gt;&gt; Well thank you for an inspiring talk.

00:46:46.670 --> 00:46:48.970
You talked about supply chains,

00:46:48.970 --> 00:46:51.550
supply chains and
there are a lot of CEOs in that.

00:46:51.550 --> 00:46:53.850
Can you talk about
sustainable consumption?

00:46:53.850 --> 00:46:57.250
So, how do we move people away from,
let's say, buying more cars.

00:46:57.250 --> 00:47:01.480
We're looking at penetration of
electric cars right now in the market.

00:47:01.480 --> 00:47:04.520
But have you thought about
where are we going to

00:47:04.520 --> 00:47:06.349
dispose of batteries in the next 30,
40 years?

00:47:08.640 --> 00:47:11.180
&gt;&gt; Well, one of the things that people
are starting to think about, obviously,

00:47:11.180 --> 00:47:12.680
is to move from this linear economy.

00:47:12.680 --> 00:47:15.450
That I briefly referred to
as this circular economy.

00:47:15.450 --> 00:47:19.170
Right now, we're basically digging
it out of the ground, producing, and

00:47:19.170 --> 00:47:20.210
then dumping it again.

00:47:20.210 --> 00:47:24.160
And if we keep digging, you end up in
Australia and there's not much left,

00:47:24.160 --> 00:47:25.780
and that's really what we are doing.

00:47:25.780 --> 00:47:29.990
So how can you be sure that someone
else's waste is someone else's input, so

00:47:29.990 --> 00:47:31.590
that nothing goes to waste?

00:47:31.590 --> 00:47:33.150
So at Unilever, we for example said,

00:47:33.150 --> 00:47:37.560
we want to run all of our 500 plus
factories that we have at zero waste.

00:47:37.560 --> 00:47:40.320
we actually achieved that
two years ahead of target

00:47:40.320 --> 00:47:41.860
by thinking about it differently.

00:47:41.860 --> 00:47:45.300
Our cost goes down, the pride goes up,
quality goes up actually.

00:47:45.300 --> 00:47:46.450
Customer service goes up.

00:47:46.450 --> 00:47:48.430
There's a lot of benefits from doing that.

00:47:48.430 --> 00:47:52.640
So, the circular economy concept thinks
about moving into that direction.

00:47:52.640 --> 00:47:55.120
It's very exciting with
Millenials actually.

00:47:55.120 --> 00:47:56.910
They are not as possessive as we were.

00:47:56.910 --> 00:47:59.650
They really are growing
up in a sharing economy.

00:47:59.650 --> 00:48:02.280
Uber, AirBnB are the examples of that.

00:48:02.280 --> 00:48:06.380
But increasingly the feel very
comfortable by paying for experiences,

00:48:06.380 --> 00:48:08.550
but not paying for physical goods.

00:48:08.550 --> 00:48:10.560
So that goes in to the right direction.

00:48:10.560 --> 00:48:13.140
But having said that,
it needs obviously much more.

00:48:13.140 --> 00:48:16.510
So in Europe now, there's a directive
on century economy coming in.

00:48:16.510 --> 00:48:19.349
Many countries in the world don't
have waste recycling systems yet.

00:48:20.420 --> 00:48:23.300
And it's difficult for
countries to do or start sometimes, but

00:48:23.300 --> 00:48:27.200
if you have a country like India or Brazil
or China not having waste recycling,

00:48:27.200 --> 00:48:29.030
then Houston we have a problem.

00:48:29.030 --> 00:48:34.280
So, we've put the global consumer goods
industry for example, together to,

00:48:34.280 --> 00:48:40.710
because no company can do it alone,
to move into some of that, in targeting

00:48:40.710 --> 00:48:46.380
country by country for example, to move
to recycling systems and other things.

00:48:46.380 --> 00:48:49.080
And at the end of the day,
we need to, which is very hard for

00:48:49.080 --> 00:48:53.780
companies to understand, we need to
stimulate consumers to use less.

00:48:53.780 --> 00:48:57.160
And people might say,
why would you advocate that as a company?

00:48:57.160 --> 00:49:00.600
Because there is a big difference
between using less material and

00:49:00.600 --> 00:49:02.630
creating more value.

00:49:02.630 --> 00:49:05.719
I can create far more
value with less material.

00:49:05.719 --> 00:49:07.646
But you have to think about that.

00:49:07.646 --> 00:49:10.223
So, we look at all of our products, for

00:49:10.223 --> 00:49:14.380
example, do complections,
do lightweighting on plastics.

00:49:15.380 --> 00:49:18.850
Be sure that actually the products
leave from what's in the bottle because

00:49:18.850 --> 00:49:22.750
In many products, 15-20% stays in
the bottle when you throw it away.

00:49:22.750 --> 00:49:26.530
And as we do all of these things you
actually get higher customer satisfaction.

00:49:26.530 --> 00:49:30.070
Create relations so
as consumers you can build value of.

00:49:30.070 --> 00:49:34.410
So you have to indeed think about
a gross model without using more stuff.

00:49:35.450 --> 00:49:37.399
But creating more value
in its broadest sense.

00:49:39.040 --> 00:49:41.370
And those models are actually
very successful.

00:49:41.370 --> 00:49:46.060
You take food and food in the US
has always been driven by calories.

00:49:46.060 --> 00:49:47.660
You would go to one of these pizza places,

00:49:47.660 --> 00:49:50.270
and I'm victim of it
myself with my three boys.

00:49:50.270 --> 00:49:52.190
I could go to Pizza Hut, and

00:49:52.190 --> 00:49:55.330
as a European I could never understand it
because I brought in two or three coupons.

00:49:55.330 --> 00:49:57.526
And I would leave with money and
five pizzas.

00:49:57.526 --> 00:49:59.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So,

00:49:59.470 --> 00:50:01.140
I never understood how the system worked.

00:50:01.140 --> 00:50:04.347
But this was the American dream, I guess.

00:50:04.347 --> 00:50:07.353
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But, it was all based on calories.

00:50:07.353 --> 00:50:11.138
And it was, the lower the better,
the more we could get calories.

00:50:11.138 --> 00:50:14.880
And that gave a certain
level of happiness, I guess.

00:50:14.880 --> 00:50:19.030
Now, you see in the food industry in the
United States rapidly moving to nutrition.

00:50:19.030 --> 00:50:21.850
People are willing to pay more for
less excess.

00:50:21.850 --> 00:50:26.430
I would go to a restaurant where I get a
half a plate, I would actually pay more so

00:50:26.430 --> 00:50:30.550
that I don't have to see all
that stuff I can't eat anyway or

00:50:30.550 --> 00:50:33.210
feel very bad that it's
thrown away afterwards.

00:50:33.210 --> 00:50:35.020
I actually pay more for it now.

00:50:35.020 --> 00:50:38.410
And I think that's happening in
the global economy in total.

00:50:38.410 --> 00:50:41.640
So, that notion of that a little
bit more purpose driven and

00:50:42.930 --> 00:50:44.600
more conscious sharing is creeping up.

00:50:46.240 --> 00:50:49.800
&gt;&gt; Paul, in these last two
minutes that we have with you,

00:50:49.800 --> 00:50:51.570
could you share some advice?

00:50:51.570 --> 00:50:56.600
Most of us here are MBA students
looking to go back in to.

00:50:56.600 --> 00:51:02.146
For profit organizations and will probably
not be the CEO right off the start,

00:51:02.146 --> 00:51:03.326
so-
&gt;&gt; You might.

00:51:03.326 --> 00:51:05.639
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:51:05.639 --> 00:51:09.965
What can we do To

00:51:09.965 --> 00:51:13.675
bring sustainable practices into
the organizations we're going to.

00:51:13.675 --> 00:51:16.425
&gt;&gt; Yeah, first of all don't have
an ambition to become a CEO that this is

00:51:16.425 --> 00:51:17.685
a stupid ambition.

00:51:17.685 --> 00:51:20.495
I always tell people not because
I don't like my job, but

00:51:20.495 --> 00:51:22.995
it's just anybody can have that title.

00:51:22.995 --> 00:51:26.865
The most important thing is
that you pursue your purpose.

00:51:26.865 --> 00:51:28.775
Whatever you feel strong about.

00:51:29.780 --> 00:51:32.530
If you figure out where you
want to make the difference,

00:51:32.530 --> 00:51:34.560
that's the first thing you need to do.

00:51:34.560 --> 00:51:37.760
Some might fight for women's rights,
some might have to fight for

00:51:37.760 --> 00:51:41.790
people in emerging markets, some might
want to include better education.

00:51:41.790 --> 00:51:44.960
But, you need to have something that
you want to have an impact in, as well.

00:51:44.960 --> 00:51:48.110
And that has to align with your values.

00:51:48.110 --> 00:51:49.600
If that aligns with your values,

00:51:49.600 --> 00:51:53.030
you are going to be successful
because it will drive your passion.

00:51:53.030 --> 00:51:58.851
Doesn't matter, the money you earn is
not an indicator of success anymore.

00:51:58.851 --> 00:52:02.915
People's self-worth should not be
measured by people's net worth.

00:52:02.915 --> 00:52:05.570
It's very important if you
want to have a good life.

00:52:05.570 --> 00:52:11.270
And then the third thing is
obviously a positive attitude

00:52:11.270 --> 00:52:16.170
is equally important because the things
never go the way you want them to go.

00:52:16.170 --> 00:52:18.720
Otherwise, someone else would
have done it before you.

00:52:18.720 --> 00:52:21.750
So the road to change is a road
that has many skeptics or

00:52:21.750 --> 00:52:26.830
cynics, rejections, but if you
are strongly driven by your purpose and

00:52:26.830 --> 00:52:31.500
your values I think, and the stronger
they are, the better beacon you have,

00:52:31.500 --> 00:52:35.410
in a very volatile environment,
to deal with these challenges.

00:52:35.410 --> 00:52:39.940
You stay guided as someone once said,
by the lights of the stars,

00:52:39.940 --> 00:52:42.460
not by the lights of the passing ships.

00:52:42.460 --> 00:52:45.640
And at the end of the day,
you need to do what you really feel you

00:52:45.640 --> 00:52:49.620
can make the biggest difference, if you're
in a unfortunate position like ours.

00:52:49.620 --> 00:52:54.260
I don't say it on camera but
the reason I work in this company and

00:52:54.260 --> 00:52:58.810
give my energy to that is because I can
have a bigger impact in doing what I do

00:52:58.810 --> 00:53:03.140
now than I can see in any other
opportunity that is offered to me.

00:53:03.140 --> 00:53:08.040
And if that is a possibility to have
a bigger impact to change the world for

00:53:08.040 --> 00:53:09.660
the better, then that's my duty to do.

00:53:10.830 --> 00:53:12.670
Until someone else can
do it better than I do.

00:53:13.800 --> 00:53:18.350
And the final point I just want
to leave you is the importance of

00:53:18.350 --> 00:53:20.900
a positive attitude.

00:53:22.080 --> 00:53:27.400
Reminds me of a story that my father
always taught me of the boiling water

00:53:27.400 --> 00:53:29.228
with the carob, the egg and
the coffee bean.

00:53:29.228 --> 00:53:33.010
And the act is actually very soft.

00:53:33.010 --> 00:53:36.660
But it goes into the boiling water and
becomes very hard and stubborn.

00:53:36.660 --> 00:53:38.560
You can't do anything with it.

00:53:38.560 --> 00:53:40.580
The carrot was actually strong.

00:53:40.580 --> 00:53:44.860
Went in to the boiling water,
but became very soft and mushy.

00:53:44.860 --> 00:53:47.520
But the coffee bean
went into the water and

00:53:47.520 --> 00:53:50.240
changed the colour of the water around it.

00:53:50.240 --> 00:53:51.845
But state the coffee bean.

00:53:51.845 --> 00:53:55.769
So, my message to all of you is
just be the coffee bean in life.

00:53:55.769 --> 00:53:59.137
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Everyone a big round of applause for

00:53:59.137 --> 00:54:00.064
Paul Coleman please.

00:54:00.064 --> 00:54:01.341
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:01.341 --> 00:54:05.820
[MUSIC]

